code_review.final_verdict:
VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED

The new `BusinessDays` utility is clean and well-placed, the membership number correctly falls out for free (the mapper reads `owner.getRegistrationDate()` at line 76, which is now the adjusted stored value), and there is no V2 `addOwner` to keep in sync. One structural issue, though:

**The "effective registration date" policy is now duplicated across two units.**

`Owner.onCreate()` (model/Owner.java:144-149):
```java
if (this.registrationDate == null) {
    this.registrationDate = LocalDate.now();
}
this.registrationDate = BusinessDays.rollToBusinessDay(this.registrationDate);
```
`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (OwnerRestControllerV1.java:117-119):
```java
LocalDate effectiveDate = BusinessDays.rollToBusinessDay(
    owner.getRegistrationDate() != null ? owner.getRegistrationDate() : LocalDate.now());
owner.setRegistrationDate(effectiveDate);
```

Both express the same compound rule — "default a null date to `LocalDate.now()`, then roll forward to a business day." Only the leaf operation (`rollToBusinessDay`) was centralized; the *default-to-now-then-roll* composition is re-implemented in the controller. The controller legitimately needs the value eagerly (for `countOwnersRegisteredOn` and, via save, the membership number), so computing it there is fine — but it should call one shared definition rather than restate the policy. If the defaulting rule ever changes (timezone, a business-aware default), the two sites can silently diverge by create path.

Concrete fix: give the one policy a single home and have both call sites use it. For example, add to `BusinessDays`:
```java
/** Resolve the effective registration date: the supplied date, or the server date when
 *  null, rolled forward onto a business day. */
public static LocalDate effectiveRegistrationDate(LocalDate supplied) {
    return rollToBusinessDay(supplied != null ? supplied : LocalDate.now());
}
```
Then `onCreate()` becomes `this.registrationDate = BusinessDays.effectiveRegistrationDate(this.registrationDate);` and the controller becomes `LocalDate effectiveDate = BusinessDays.effectiveRegistrationDate(owner.getRegistrationDate());`. (Placing it on `Owner` as an instance helper would work equally well; `BusinessDays` keeps the model dependency-free.) This preserves the idempotent `@PrePersist` backstop for non-controller paths while removing the duplicated policy.

